---
// A low-profile laptop frame around ScreenMock's schematic screenshot. Two
// shapes, stacked: a bezel enclosing the screen on all 4 sides (so it reads
// as a closed display, not a stray top/bottom rule), and below it a base bar
// that's visibly WIDER than the bezel and protrudes past it on both sides —
// the one silhouette cue that reads as "laptop" rather than "monitor": a
// screen sitting on top of a wider base. Deliberately not a literal
// keyboard rendering — see the base's own comment below. An earlier version
// used two same-width horizontal bars (top + bottom) with no side bezel;
// screenshotting it at real page scale showed a plain screen with a header
// and footer bar, not a device — the enclosing frame and the wider base are
// what a monitor lacks and a laptop has.
//
// This stays outside ScreenMock itself — that component deliberately draws
// no bezel of its own (see its own comment: it stands for the pixels a
// screen shows, not a framed photo of one). The frame lives here instead,
// one layer further out, so ScreenMock's own "no inset" argument still
// holds for the mock itself.
//
// The bezel border grows outward from ScreenMock's own fixed width (border
// is outside the content box), and the base is wider still — both overflow
// past the 560px/432px width CodePanel and ScreenMock otherwise share. That
// horizontal growth is deliberate here (unlike the old same-width bars): a
// laptop base overhanging its screen is the point, not a bug to budget away.
// DemoColumn sits at the end of the hero's `1fr auto` row with room to
// spare, so the overflow lands in that slack rather than causing a
// horizontal scrollbar — verified against tests/landing.spec.ts's
// never-scrolls-horizontally check across every breakpoint.
//
// gray-700, not gray-600 or gray-800: gray-600 (oklch 0.4) reads as brushed
// aluminum, not matte black. gray-800 (oklch 0.175) reads as matte black but
// turned out too close to the wallpaper's own darkness (0.13) to survive the
// wallpaper's diagonal line texture — confirmed by sampling the rendered
// side bezel at 4x zoom against the wallpaper and finding the two nearly
// indistinguishable there, even with a 1px line-2 (16%-alpha white) edge
// ring. gray-700 (0.26) is the value that stayed dark enough to read as
// "matte charcoal plastic" while still holding a real contrast margin
// against the 0.13 wallpaper on all 4 sides, not just the sides that happen
// to sit next to a plainer part of the wallpaper. The edge ring itself also
// moved from line-2 to ink-3 (opaque, not 16%-alpha) for the same reason: an
// alpha line blends into whatever's behind it, an opaque one doesn't.
//
// The deck is a plain flat bar — no key ticks, no trackpoint square. Both
// were tried and both dropped: the tick pattern read as a barcode rather
// than keys (see git history), and a trackpoint with no keys around it was
// an inconsistent detail on its own. More fundamentally, this whole mockup
// draws the screen face-on; a literal top-down keyboard surface doesn't
// belong in the same flat view as a face-on screen — that combination is
// the "perspective is broken" a real 3D or isometric laptop render would
// need to reconcile, which this schematic, flat-color site deliberately
// doesn't attempt. The deck stays purely abstract: "there's a wider chassis
// under here," nothing claiming to be a literal rendering of its surface.
// The enclosing bezel plus the wider base alone already carry the "laptop,
// not monitor" read (confirmed separately) without needing any surface
// detail to back it up.
//
// shadow-panel (folded into the same edge-ring shadow) lifts each shape off
// the wallpaper the same way CodePanel already does above — free "this is a
// physical object" weight, since a box-shadow costs no layout space to
// compensate for.
//
// data-physical-object="lid"/"base" round each shape's own corners — the
// site's one deliberate exception to radius-0 (see ADR 0030's amendment):
// this mockup is standing in for a real object next to the screen it wraps,
// not more UI chrome, and a sharp-cornered frame around a sharp-cornered
// ScreenMock had no visual signal telling the two apart. The rounding is
// asymmetric on purpose, the way a real laptop's is: the lid rounds only
// its top corners (away from the hinge), the base only its bottom corners
// (the front edge resting on the desk) — the hinge line where the two meet
// stays flush (radius 0) on both, since that's not a corner a real hinge
// has. overflow-hidden on the bezel clips ScreenMock's own sharp corners to
// match its now-rounded top — border-radius rounds a border's own
// rendering but doesn't clip already-sharp content inside it.
//
// The base is noticeably taller than the bezel border (12px vs 6px, not
// matched 1:1) — an earlier version matched the bezel's own 6px, which read
// as a thin trim rather than a chassis with real depth. A laptop base is
// physically thicker than its screen bezel; this gives that some visual
// weight without growing so tall it threatens the above-fold budget below.
//
// data-screen-glass is a radial vignette layered over ScreenMock, inside the
// bezel — the same technique crt.css's own .crt-vignette already uses on
// this site (radial-gradient, transparent center, dark oklch edges), just
// scoped to one screen instead of the whole viewport. crt-vignette's own
// 0.37 alpha, copied verbatim, read as basically invisible here — pixel-
// sampled the rendered corners to confirm rather than re-guess, and the
// value that actually reads at normal page scale (0.4, tighter ellipse,
// earlier transparent-to-dark transition) is closer to crt-vignette's than
// the first, too-cautious attempt was. A full-viewport effect stays legible
// at low alpha because it's already large; the same alpha over a ~300px
// area needs to work harder to register at all. This is glass sitting over
// ScreenMock, not ScreenMock drawing itself differently, so its own
// "stands for pixels, not a photo of pixels" argument still holds — the
// vignette lives in this component's own physical-object layer instead.
// It's wrapped in its own plain `relative` div rather than making the
// bezel itself `relative` — the bezel carries a box-shadow, and promoting
// it to a positioned element made it paint (shadow included) *above* its
// static `base` sibling regardless of DOM order, instead of behind it like
// before. Positioned elements always paint after non-positioned siblings in
// the same stacking context, with or without z-index — confirmed by
// bounding-box measurement that the layout itself was never wrong, only
// the paint order after `relative` was added.
//
// Decorative only, same as the screen it wraps — aria-hidden, no focusable
// content.
---

<div aria-hidden="true" class="flex flex-col items-center">
  <div
    data-physical-object="lid"
    class="overflow-hidden border-[6px] border-gray-700 shadow-[0_0_0_1px_var(--color-ink-3),var(--shadow-panel)] tight:border-[4px]"
  >
    <div class="relative">
      <slot />
      <div data-screen-glass class="absolute inset-0"></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div
    data-physical-object="base"
    class="h-[12px] w-[588px] bg-gray-700 shadow-[0_0_0_1px_var(--color-ink-3),var(--shadow-panel)] tight:h-[8px] tight:w-[456px]"
  >
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* A data attribute, not a class, so this stays outside the Tailwind
     class-layer contract (see check-class-layers.ts) — the same convention
     [data-reveal] (DemoColumn.astro) already uses. */
  [data-screen-glass] {
    background-image: radial-gradient(
      ellipse 70% 60% at 50% 42%,
      transparent 30%,
      oklch(0 0 0 / 0.4) 100%
    );
  }
</style>
